  "pride_metadata": {
    "project": {
      "accession": "PXD038689",
      "title": "The Arabidopsis tyrosine phosphatase RLPH2 targets D-group MAP kinases",
      "additionalAttributes": [],
      "projectDescription": "Knowing that biochemically RLPH2 is a tyrosine phosphatase, we selectively In an attempt to discover RLPH2 substrates we decided to use the power of phospho-proteomics. Immunoprecipitated tyrosine phosphorylated peptides from enriched phospho-peptide pools derived from these plant extracts. Mass spectrometry analysis revealed a dramatic enrichment of tyrosine phosphorylated peptides derived from the activation loops of a subset of Arabidopsis mitogen activated protein kinases (MPKs), specifically the D-group enzymes.",
      "sampleProcessingProtocol": "Protein extraction and digestion  Wild type and AtRLPH2 knockout (atrlph2-1 and atrlph2-2) plants were grown as in 2.2.3. Rosette leaves were harvested, flash frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at -80\u00baC until day of use. All steps were performed at room temperature (RT) unless otherwise stated. Protein extraction and digestion were performed by Filter Aided Sample Preparation (FASP) (Wisniewski, 2017; Uhrig et al 2019).  Peptide isolation  Peptides were retained after centrifugation at 4,000 xg for 20 min. Next 3 mL of ABC buffer was added to the concentrator and centrifuged for 20 min followed by the addition of 3 mL of 500 mM NaCl and centrifugation at 4,000 xg for 45 min. Peptides were acidified with 25% (v/v) trifluoracetic acid (TFA) to a final concentration of 0.5% (v/v).  Peptide desalting  Peptides were desalted with Sep-Pak light C18 cartridges (Waters). First, the cartridge was washed with 1 mL MeOH and 1 mL of elution buffer (60% (v/v) acetonitrile (ACN), 0.15% (v/v) TFA) followed by equilibration with 3 x 1 mL binding buffer (3% (v/v) ACN, 0.1% (v/v) TFA). Next, the peptide sample from 4.2.1.2 was loaded and bound sample was washed twice with 1 mL binding buffer. Peptides were eluted with 2 x 1 mL elution buffer. Eluates were made to a final concentration of 80% (v/v) ACN and 6% (v/v) TFA to prepare peptides for phospho-enrichment.  TiO2 Phospho-peptide & phospho-tyrosine peptide enrichment  Phosphopeptide enrichment was performed as previously described (Uhrig et al 2019). Once dried, phosphopeptides were resuspended in 800 \u03bcL ice-cold IP buffer (50 mM HEPES-NaOH pH 7.4, 50 mM NaCl). Solution pH was adjusted to 7.5 and incubated end-over-end, overnight at 4\u00baC with 50 \u03bcL of phospho-tyrosine antibody agarose beads (Santa Cruz) and 15 \u03bcL of phospho-tyrosine antibody Sepharose beads (p-Tyr-100, Cell Signaling) pre-equilibrated in IP buffer. Beads were pelleted and washed 3 times for 2 min at RT with 1 mL IP buffer followed by 3 washes with ddH2O. Tyrosine phosphorylated peptides were eluted with 3x 200 \u03bcL of 80% (v/v) ACN, 2% (v/v) formic acid and eluates were dried with speed vacuum and kept at -20\u00baC.  Mass spectrometry and analysis of results  Dry peptide samples were re-suspended in 3% ACN / 0.1% TFA and desalted using C18 ZipTips (Sigma; Z720046). Eluted phosphopeptide samples were then dried and re-suspended in 10 \u03bcL of 3% ACN / 0.1% FA. Dissolved samples (4 \u03bcL) were injected by an Easy-nLC 1000 system (Thermo) and separated on a 15 cm EasySpray column (ES902; Thermo). The column was equilibrated with 100% solvent A (0.1% FA in H20). Peptides were eluted using the following gradient of solvent B (0.1% FA in ACN): 0-50 min; 0-25% B, 50-60 min; 25-32% B, 60-70 min; 32-98% B at a flow rate of 0.3 \u03bcl/min. High accuracy mass spectra were acquired with an Orbitrap Fusion (Thermo) that was operated in data-dependent acquisition mode. All precursor signals were recorded in the Orbitrap using quadrupole transmission in the mass range of 300-1500 m/z. Spectra were recorded with a resolution of 120 000 at 190 m/z, a target value of 4E5 and the maximum cycle time was set to 3 seconds. Data-dependent MS/MS were recorded in the linear ion trap using quadrupole isolation with a window of 2 Da and HCD fragmentation with 30% fragmentation energy. The ion trap was operated in rapid scan mode with a target value of 3E4 and a maximum injection time of 100 ms. Precursor signals were selected for fragmentation with a charge state from +2 to +7 and a signal intensity of at least 1E4. A dynamic exclusion list was used for 30 seconds and maximum parallelizing ion injections was activated.",
      "dataProcessingProtocol": "Protein extraction and digestion  Wild type and AtRLPH2 knockout (atrlph2-1 and atrlph2-2) plants were grown as in 2.2.3. Rosette leaves were harvested, flash frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at -80\u00baC until day of use. All steps were performed at room temperature (RT) unless otherwise stated. Protein extraction and digestion were performed by Filter Aided Sample Preparation (FASP) (Wisniewski, 2017; Uhrig et al 2019).  Peptide isolation  Peptides were retained after centrifugation at 4,000 xg for 20 min. Next 3 mL of ABC buffer was added to the concentrator and centrifuged for 20 min followed by the addition of 3 mL of 500 mM NaCl and centrifugation at 4,000 xg for 45 min. Peptides were acidified with 25% (v/v) trifluoracetic acid (TFA) to a final concentration of 0.5% (v/v).  Peptide desalting  Peptides were desalted with Sep-Pak light C18 cartridges (Waters). First, the cartridge was washed with 1 mL MeOH and 1 mL of elution buffer (60% (v/v) acetonitrile (ACN), 0.15% (v/v) TFA) followed by equilibration with 3 x 1 mL binding buffer (3% (v/v) ACN, 0.1% (v/v) TFA). Next, the peptide sample from 4.2.1.2 was loaded and bound sample was washed twice with 1 mL binding buffer. Peptides were eluted with 2 x 1 mL elution buffer. Eluates were made to a final concentration of 80% (v/v) ACN and 6% (v/v) TFA to prepare peptides for phospho-enrichment.  TiO2 Phospho-peptide & phospho-tyrosine peptide enrichment  Phosphopeptide enrichment was performed as previously described (Uhrig et al 2019). Once dried, phosphopeptides were resuspended in 800 \u03bcL ice-cold IP buffer (50 mM HEPES-NaOH pH 7.4, 50 mM NaCl). Solution pH was adjusted to 7.5 and incubated end-over-end, overnight at 4\u00baC with 50 \u03bcL of phospho-tyrosine antibody agarose beads (Santa Cruz) and 15 \u03bcL of phospho-tyrosine antibody Sepharose beads (p-Tyr-100, Cell Signaling) pre-equilibrated in IP buffer. Beads were pelleted and washed 3 times for 2 min at RT with 1 mL IP buffer followed by 3 washes with ddH2O. Tyrosine phosphorylated peptides were eluted with 3x 200 \u03bcL of 80% (v/v) ACN, 2% (v/v) formic acid and eluates were dried with speed vacuum and kept at -20\u00baC.  Mass spectrometry and analysis of results  Dry peptide samples were re-suspended in 3% ACN / 0.1% TFA and desalted using C18 ZipTips (Sigma; Z720046). Eluted phosphopeptide samples were then dried and re-suspended in 10 \u03bcL of 3% ACN / 0.1% FA. Dissolved samples (4 \u03bcL) were injected by an Easy-nLC 1000 system (Thermo) and separated on a 15 cm EasySpray column (ES902; Thermo). The column was equilibrated with 100% solvent A (0.1% FA in H20). Peptides were eluted using the following gradient of solvent B (0.1% FA in ACN): 0-50 min; 0-25% B, 50-60 min; 25-32% B, 60-70 min; 32-98% B at a flow rate of 0.3 \u03bcl/min. High accuracy mass spectra were acquired with an Orbitrap Fusion (Thermo) that was operated in data-dependent acquisition mode. All precursor signals were recorded in the Orbitrap using quadrupole transmission in the mass range of 300-1500 m/z. Spectra were recorded with a resolution of 120 000 at 190 m/z, a target value of 4E5 and the maximum cycle time was set to 3 seconds. Data-dependent MS/MS were recorded in the linear ion trap using quadrupole isolation with a window of 2 Da and HCD fragmentation with 30% fragmentation energy. The ion trap was operated in rapid scan mode with a target value of 3E4 and a maximum injection time of 100 ms. Precursor signals were selected for fragmentation with a charge state from +2 to +7 and a signal intensity of at least 1E4. A dynamic exclusion list was used for 30 seconds and maximum parallelizing ion injections was activated.",
